    "sessionId": "claude-readability",
    "text": " Why long replies read dense on the phone\n\nThe stack draws every item at the same weight, so a long answer and a one-line tool row compete for attention. Three things make it worse:\n\n- Body text is 15pt with tight line spacing: fine for a sentence, tiring for a page.\n- Tool rows sit flush against prose, with no gap that says the agent stopped talking here.\n- Inline code like Palette.raised renders at body size, so it reads louder than the words around it.\n\n What I changed\n\n1. Raised paragraph spacing in MarkdownView from 6 to 10 points.\n2. Gave a tool row a 12pt top margin when it follows an assistant message.\n3. Dropped inline code to 0.92em and tinted its background.\n\nThe numbers live in one place now:\n\nswift\nenum Spacing {\n    static let paragraph: CGFloat = 10\n    static let toolAfterProse: CGFloat = 12\n    static let inlineCodeScale: CGFloat = 0.92\n}\n\n\n Before and after\n\n Surface  Before  After \n ---  ---  --- \n Paragraph gap  6pt  10pt \n Tool row after prose  0pt  12pt \n Inline code  1.0em  0.92em \n\nMeasured at the default Dynamic Type size; larger sizes scale with it.\n\n Where to look\n\n- The reasoning is in the [architecture notes](docs/architecture.md), under Publishing.\n- Apple's guidance: [Human Interface Guidelines: Typography](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/typography).\n- The renderer is mobile/conch-ios/conch-ios/MarkdownView.swift, and the stack that spaces items is ConversationStack.swift.\n\nSource guards pass. Want the same spacing on the Mac pane?",
    "spokenChars": 0,
    "markdown": "## Why long replies read dense on the phone\n\nThe stack draws every item at the same weight, so a long answer and a one-line tool row compete for attention. Three things make it worse:\n\n- **Body text is 15pt** with tight line spacing: fine for a sentence, tiring for a page.\n- Tool rows sit flush against prose, with no gap that says *the agent stopped talking here*.\n- Inline code like `Palette.raised` renders at body size, so it reads louder than the words around it.\n\n### What I changed\n\n1. Raised paragraph spacing in `MarkdownView` from 6 to 10 points.\n2. Gave a tool row a 12pt top margin when it follows an assistant message.\n3. Dropped inline code to 0.92em and tinted its background.\n\nThe numbers live in one place now:\n\n```swift\nenum Spacing {\n    static let paragraph: CGFloat = 10\n    static let toolAfterProse: CGFloat = 12\n    static let inlineCodeScale: CGFloat = 0.92\n}\n```\n\n### Before and after\n\n| Surface | Before | After |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Paragraph gap | 6pt | 10pt |\n| Tool row after prose | 0pt | 12pt |\n| Inline code | 1.0em | 0.92em |\n\nMeasured at the default Dynamic Type size; larger sizes scale with it.\n\n### Where to look\n\n- The reasoning is in the [architecture notes](docs/architecture.md), under *Publishing*.\n- Apple's guidance: [Human Interface Guidelines: Typography](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/typography).\n- The renderer is mobile/conch-ios/conch-ios/MarkdownView.swift, and the stack that spaces items is `ConversationStack.swift`.\n\nSource guards pass. Want the same spacing on the Mac pane?"
  },
